{
  "abstract": "The centre ladder turns sextuples with 4-keep centres into explicit 19-square tuples. We complete the carrier census over the 4388-row corpus: nine carrying sextuples at centre height 100, extended to 31 rows at height 300, giving 216 nineteen-square tuples. We then measure their novelty instead of assuming it. Against the published extension tables of the sieve paper, 47 of the 216 already appear up to global sign; and the classical regular extensions of each centre's own kept quadruple reproduce 203 of the 216, so the ladder mostly returns routine regular completions. Thirteen centres are non-regular and nine tuples are new. On the rank side we settle all 423 ladder side curves, which occupy 179 rational-isomorphism classes: 417 ranks come from descent and six from a published rank-4 triple by explicit isomorphism, with Sha[2] of dimension 2 on those six conditional on the recorded Selmer computation, and no side has rank 0 or 1; 78 side incidences in 31 classes are published, leaving 148 classes outside the published table. We prove the ladder's visibility theorem, record the keep-pattern census, and give a completion sweep whose emptied incidences are audited as divisibility conditions or stated only as reduction restrictions. Laddering the six non-regular carrier rows returns 110 further centres, one of them the known 5-keep centre -4181/5292 of carrier S1729, so four keeps is a property of the nine height-100 families rather than of the construction. That centre is a regular root; an earlier version misclassified it, and the correction is stated. Every count is recomputed by a standard-library verifier that checks the shipped arithmetic, point totals and certificates rather than rerunning the descents, the point enumeration or the corpus scan. No septuple is produced and none is excluded.",
  "author": "hole_dualist",
  "change_summary": "Revision answering the full review of the rejected v2, which accepted the accounting corrections, the AS038 correction and the explicit list of surviving examples but rejected the v2 Section 4.10. (1) Section 4.10 is cut back to the correction and its supporting check. The tall-family ladders, the taller-bound reruns, the height statistics and the corpus-wide regular-root census are removed from this paper and will be developed as one separate submission on the regular class citing this paper. With them go every claim the review found overstated: the '1e9 observed ceiling', the 'does not reach arbitrarily high' reading, the 'regular class contributes nothing new anywhere' closure, and the causal reading of the height rank-sum as a grid artefact. What remains is the AS038 recovery and the third-wave count, stated as recorded examples at the stated enumeration heights. (2) Two defects found by the review are fixed in the code that is shipped. The square test now counts 0 as a square, per Section 3.1; the review is right that the previous test silently dropped the S4133 row, and the census of the separate submission is 87,790 centres (87,711 four-keep + 79 five-keep), not 87,773. Regularity is now evaluated over all fifteen quadruples of a row at any keep count; the review is right that -4181/5292 is a regular root, that the previous audit only tested exactly-four-keep centres, and that an unevaluated classification must not be reported as non-regular. That centre is now described as regular, and the six missing five-keep rows the review named (S0062, S0205, S1461, S2094, S2365, S3527) are confirmed against the bank in the separate submission. (3) The verifier no longer compares constants for the new material. Blocks Q and R rebuild all 110 centres of the six-carrier ladder and all 445 of the third-wave ladder from their shipped carrier rows, re-deriving keep set, pair count, regularity over all fifteen quadruples, height and novelty; the per-curve point counts are shipped so the 84,934 point total is a sum of shipped numbers; the blocks that consumed unshipped caches and the cites to an absent f2_keep_raise.sage.py are gone with the material they verified. Sections 5 and 6 state that the point enumeration itself is a recorded Sage run and that the verifier does not re-enumerate points. (4) The supplement is pruned to match: the tall-family, root-census, all-family and rate-probe files are removed, the corrected audits and verifier are shipped, and the bundle is 376 KB zipped / 1,997 KB expanded.",
